Show other clients' windows under the application's

Cage shows one application. With -u it goes on showing that application
in front, and with the keyboard, when other clients connect: their
windows are put behind it instead of over it. Where the application's
window is transparent they are seen, and where it also leaves a point out
of its input region they get the pointer and touches there.

A window shown this way is given, in place of the whole screen, the part
of it that the application's window does not cover - by the application's
own account, which is its opaque region. An application that draws bars
along the top and bottom of the screen and says that those are opaque has
the other windows fitted between them.

One such window is shown at a time, and none to begin with. Which, is for
the application to say, with the wlr-foreign-toplevel-management protocol
that Cage now offers: every window is announced with its title and
application id, the activate request shows a window in place of any other,
and the minimize request hides it. A hidden window is not drawn.

This is what lets the OpenGVA HMI be the one screen the crew sees, with
another program's picture - a sight's video, a map - in the middle of it.

Without -u nothing changes but that the protocol is offered, and that its
activate request brings a window to the front.

Upstream: not submitted

